            <ArticleTitle>Obesity in Pregnancy: Optimizing Outcomes for Mom and Baby.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>87-106</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0025-7125(17)30132-3</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.mcna.2017.08.008</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Obesity is common in women of childbearing age, and management of this population around the time of pregnancy involves specific challenges. Weight and medical comorbidities should be optimized both before and during pregnancy. During pregnancy, gestational weight gain should be limited, comorbidities should be appropriately screened for and managed, and fetal health should be monitored. Consideration should be given to the optimal timing of delivery and to reducing surgical and anesthetic complications. In the postpartum period, breastfeeding and weight loss should be promoted. Maternal obesity is associated with adverse metabolic effects in offspring, promoting an intergenerational cycle of obesity.</AbstractText>
